The `run_command` function takes a command string as an argument. It then uses the fork system call to create a new process. If the fork fails, it returns 0 indicating failure.

If the current process is the child (i.e., the return value of fork is 0), it calls the execl function to replace its code with that of the specified command. If the exec fails, it also prints an error message and returns 0.

Otherwise, if the current process is the parent (i.e., the return value of fork is the pid of the child), it waits for the child to terminate using waitpid. If the child exited normally, and its exit status was 0, then it returns 1 indicating success. Otherwise, it returns 0 indicating failure.
